predef: "l2src/lmx.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_arena.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_scratch.h.lm1"
predef: "l2src/lmx_plan.h.lm1"

# lmx_walk.h - the walk of a callable's body tree: one activation of the graph interpreter.
#
# PROTOTYPE, int slice, dev/l2src_sandbox only.  lmx_interp_run stays the entry that
# recognises a callable; this unit is what gives a body VALUES: own state, declared
# inputs, results, checkpoints.  It is written to be called from lmx_interp, not to
# replace it.
#
# What is fixed by the author and by the native path, and only carried out here:
#   * the body is M itself after child[0], in order; every `return:` is a node of that
#     tree and there may be many (lmx.h.lm1, LmxCallable; dev/graph_interp/README.md);
#   * the graph is binary and carries no names; the address->name table is never read
#     (LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md, "Бинарный граф без имён");
#   * entering an interpreted method never copies M, its body or the graph; an
#     activation keeps the same choice of what is local, the same own load and the same
#     dirty behaviour as native code (LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md, "Тело метода общее,
#     активация на стеке" and the entry on stack activation).  A recursive call is a
#     recursive C call of lmx_walk_run on the same published M.  What an activation needs
#     beyond its C frame -- the records of its own fields, the values of a callee's
#     declared inputs -- is taken from the execution context's temporary-frame store
#     (lmx_scratch.h.lm1) between a mark and a rewind: this unit allocates nothing and
#     owns nothing, and after warm-up an entry allocates nothing at all.  The only thing
#     an activation writes into the graph is the publication of a dirty own value (and
#     whatever the program itself writes through an explicit field);
#   * what carries identity in the executable graph (LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md, "Приоритет
#     ссылок в исполняемом графе"): as many PHYSICAL REFERENCES as possible, a numeric
#     code only where a physical reference is impossible, text nowhere.  So a node refers
#     to its role, to the Structure that holds a field and to the callee M by address;
#     the copier remaps those references with the rest of M's tree, so a copied M runs
#     unchanged on its own state.  Numbers remain only where there is nothing to point
#     at: the slot of a field inside its Structure (slot storage is not a graph value,
#     lmx_graph_copy_owned.lm1) and the ordinal of a declared input, which lives on the
#     stack of the activation and not in the graph;
#   * own fields the body uses are loaded at entry into activation-local working values,
#     dirty is set only by an executed assignment, dirty values are published before
#     every outbound call and every exit, clean ones are never republished, nothing is
#     reloaded after a call (Lingvamyxa_spec.txt 21.5, 21.6; lmx_own.lm1);
#   * one exit order for every `return:`: evaluate the argument -> dirty-only checkpoint
#     in the lexical order of the fields -> return (l2trans.lm1 l2_emit_ret_tr, `return`
#     in l2_emit_body; LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md, "Поля Lmx лексические").
#
# The shape of a body node is Grok's (lmx.h.lm1, LmxOp): a FRAME node is an ordinary
# Structure whose child[0] refers to its ROLE and whose remaining children are its
# operands, in order.  The role is a physical reference: ONE shared record per role
# (lmx_walk_roles_open), and a node is recognised by COMPARING the address in its
# child[0] with the program's role records -- no classification of the role, no number,
# no text.  A Structure whose child[0] is some OTHER op record is a step of a role this
# program does not have: it is refused, never skipped.  A bare role record as a child,
# for the role `ret`, is a void return.
#
# PREPARATION AND ENTRY (steps/l3-activation-plan.md).  Which own fields a body uses, and
# in what lexical order, is a static property of the callable; native code gets it from
# the translator once.  Here lmx_walk_prepare gets it once: it looks at the whole body the
# way entry used to on every activation -- refuses what is unsupported or malformed
# anywhere in M, finds the distinct own fields the body names, orders them lexically,
# writes down each one's position from M -- and publishes that as the immutable
# activation plan in LmxCallable.header (lmx_plan.h.lm1).  It is this unit's job and not
# the generic plan unit's because only the owner of the body roles knows where a role
# keeps its operands.
#
# ENTRY then does not look at the body at all.  It asks the arena exactly TWICE -- is M a
# Structure, is its child[0] an LmxCallable -- compares one address (the plan's root role
# against the context's plan roles), and loads one record per entry of the plan through
# lmx_plan_slot_known, which asks nothing.  A body without a plan is LMX_WALK_UNSUPPORTED;
# it is never analysed at entry and never handed to a native entry.  The plan is trusted
# for as long as the occurrence keeps that descriptor in child[0]: the executable shape of
# an occurrence is immutable under its descriptor, and an act that changes the shape of
# one occurrence first gives that occurrence a fresh LmxCallable with no plan.
#
# What is still asked of the arena during execution, and why.  The fields of a body are,
# in lexical order, own cells and frame nodes mixed, so telling a Structure from a cell
# there costs one question per field each time the body runs; a bare role record is
# recognised before that, by its address.  The OPERANDS of a frame node sit at positions
# the role fixes and are put there by whoever builds the graph, so they are read as nodes
# without asking -- the precondition the *_known accessors state -- and a callee named by
# a call is an operand too: its own entry is what asks.  Every question of this unit goes
# through lmx_walk_kind and is counted in LmxWalkContext.classified, so a test can state
# the cost of an entry exactly rather than estimate it.
#
# Do not unify two things that look alike.  Inside a body, a node names the Structure
# that holds a field BY ADDRESS, and that is right because the copier remaps the address
# with the copy.  The activation plan, shared by every occurrence of one callable, must
# name the same fields by RELATIVE position and never by address.  A BODY (of `if`, of `while`) is a plain Structure whose children are, in
# order, own cells and frame nodes -- the same shape as M after child[0].
#
# VALUES.  A value of an activation is always a PHYSICAL REFERENCE (`@: void`): to a
# Structure, to a cell of the graph, or to a destination cell its consumer handed down.
# There is no code that says what a value is.  An integer the activation COMPUTED is
# written into the destination cell passed from above and the value is the address of
# that cell, so "is this a computed integer" is answered by physical identity with the
# destination; what a reference into the graph is, is answered by the arena's index, and
# asked only where the kind really has to be established -- an input crossing the public
# boundary, the holder that `of` reads a field from.  No value is absence of a reference,
# reported as such (LmxWalkValue.present).  Consequences, all of them the point:
#   * a Structure handed in as an input (a subject, a candidate) is never copied and is
#     handed on, returned or read through by its own address;
#   * a literal, a field read by `at` or `of`, and a passed-through input are the graph's
#     own cells, by reference -- reading a value copies nothing;
#   * integers are computed destination-passing: the consumer of a node owns the cell,
#     an automatic C int or a cell of the scratch slice of a call, so a temporary never
#     outlives the rewind of the frame that made it.  A reference into the scratch store
#     that would LEAVE an activation through `ret` is copied -- four bytes, by value --
#     into the destination of the caller; a reference into the graph leaves as it is;
#   * the inputs of a callee are an array of references in the caller's scratch slice.
# Own fields keep their int working values (LmxWalkOwn): a read copies the working value
# into the destination, because the field may be assigned again before the value is used.
#
#   ret   [ret, value?]               a return; no value for a void callable
#   call  [call, M, arg ...]          M is the callee occurrence itself, by reference
#   lit   [lit, cell]                 the int cell that holds the value, by reference
#   own   [own, holder, slot]         an own field of the running M: `holder` is M or a
#                                     body lexically inside M, by reference; `slot` is a
#                                     size_t cell.  Loaded at entry, cached, dirty-tracked
#   arg   [arg, k]                    k-th declared input of this activation (size_t cell):
#                                     the reference the caller passed, as it is
#   of    [of, value, slot]           a field of a REFERENCE VALUE: `value` is a node whose
#                                     value must be a Structure (asked of the arena);
#                                     the result is what that slot holds, by reference
#   at    [at, holder, slot]          explicit graph field read: every execution, never
#                                     cached (21.5); `holder` is any Structure, by reference
#   put   [put, holder, slot, value]  explicit graph field write, never cached
#   set   [set, own-node, value]      executed assignment of an own field: sets dirty
#   add sub lt eq  [op, a, b]
#   if    [if, cond, then, else?]     then / else are bodies
#   while [while, cond, body]
#
# The set of roles beyond RETURN and CALL and the operands of each are a carrier draft
# for probing the mechanism, not an L3 ISA (dev/graph_interp/BODY_NODES.md).
#
# Limits of this slice, stated rather than hidden: a value is an integer, a reference or
# nothing; arithmetic and own fields are int only, and whether a reference an integer
# node reads IS an int cell is the producer's proof, not checked here; a return carries
# 0 or 1 value; no throw, no poll
# (lmx_msg_poll_escape), no arrays, no char/size_t/pointer own fields, no dynamic
# inputs of 21.3, and `header` of LmxCallable is not read.
#
# What is outside the slice is REPORTED, never skipped and never handed to native code
# (docs/LMX_semantics.ru.md, #l3-receiver: a restricted profile reports an unsupported
# construct explicitly; an implementation address does not make an operation
# admissible).  A frame node whose LmxOp code this unit does not read makes the whole
# activation LMX_WALK_UNSUPPORTED at ENTRY, before any step runs and wherever in M the
# node sits, a branch never taken included.  There is no fallback to METHOD.addr: a
# callable with no body node is LMX_WALK_NO_GRAPH and that is all.

define: LMX_WALK_OK 0
# Malformed body tree, not a callable, a field reference that selects nothing, an own
# field that is not lexically inside M, a missing input.
define: LMX_WALK_INVALID 1
# A callable with no body node: nothing to walk.
define: LMX_WALK_NO_GRAPH 2
define: LMX_WALK_NOMEM 3
# A dirty own value could not be published (21.6: checkpoint failure, never silent).
define: LMX_WALK_CHECKPOINT 4
# A body node this slice does not read, or a body whose descriptor has no activation
# plan (lmx_walk_prepare was not run for it).  Nothing of the activation has run.
define: LMX_WALK_UNSUPPORTED 5

# What lmx_walk_op answers for a frame node whose code is not one of those below.
define: LMX_WALK_OP_UNKNOWN -1
# The discriminators of this slice's roles: the index of a role's record in the roles
# Structure and the `code` of that record.  They are not the identity of a node -- the
# address of the record is -- and they are not an L3 instruction set.
define: LMX_WALK_OP_NONE 0
define: LMX_WALK_OP_RET 1
define: LMX_WALK_OP_CALL 2
define: LMX_WALK_OP_LIT 3
define: LMX_WALK_OP_OWN 4
define: LMX_WALK_OP_ARG 5
define: LMX_WALK_OP_AT 6
define: LMX_WALK_OP_PUT 7
define: LMX_WALK_OP_SET 8
define: LMX_WALK_OP_ADD 9
define: LMX_WALK_OP_SUB 10
define: LMX_WALK_OP_LT 11
define: LMX_WALK_OP_EQ 12
define: LMX_WALK_OP_IF 13
define: LMX_WALK_OP_WHILE 14
define: LMX_WALK_OP_OF 15
define: LMX_WALK_OP_COUNT 16

# What a direct caller gets back.  `ref` is the value, 0 when there is none; `present` says
# so explicitly.  `number` is the destination cell this record lends the activation: when
# the value is an integer the activation computed, `ref` points AT `number` -- that
# identity, not a code, is how the caller knows.  Any other `ref` is a reference into the
# graph (or into the permanent store), to be classified by whoever needs its kind.
struct: LmxWalkValue
    @: void ref
    int: number
    int: present
end: LmxWalkValue

# One own field of the running M as this activation sees it: the slot it was loaded from
# (the publication target), the working value, and the dirty state of 21.6.  The records
# of an activation are kept in the lexical order of the fields (LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md,
# "Поля Lmx лексические"), found by walking M's own tree in order at entry; that is the
# order of every checkpoint -- not the order in which the body happens to mention them.
struct: LmxWalkOwn
    @@: void from
    int: value
    int: dirty
end: LmxWalkOwn

# What graph mode needs from its surroundings, for one Message on one thread: the
# Message's arena (to tell a Structure from a cell among the fields of a body), the
# execution context's temporary-frame store, and the program's role records.  Whoever
# runs the turn builds it; this unit only reads it.
struct: LmxWalkContext
    @: LmxArena arena
    @: LmxScratch scratch
    @: Lmx roles
    # The role records of the plan namespace (lmx_plan_roles_open): entry checks that a
    # header is a plan of THIS program by comparing one address with them.
    @: Lmx plan_roles
    # How many times this unit has asked the arena's index what an address is.
    size_t: classified
end: LmxWalkContext

# One activation.  An automatic object of lmx_walk_run; nothing of it is in the graph.
# `found` and `owns` are slices of the context's store, taken after this activation's
# mark and gone with its rewind.
struct: LmxWalkFrame
    @: LmxWalkContext context
    @: LmxArena arena
    @: Lmx roles
    @: Lmx node
    # The declared inputs: references, in the caller's storage.
    @@: void args
    size_t: nargs
    # The destination cell of the caller, for an integer this activation returns.
    @: void dest
    @: LmxWalkOwn owns
    @: LmxWalkOwn found
    size_t: nowns
    size_t: nfound
    size_t: cap
    # Preparation only: the indexes from M down to the slot being visited, how deep the
    # tree of M goes, and the position written down for each own field.  All in the store.
    @: size_t path
    size_t: depth
    @@: size_t paths
    @: size_t lens
    int: counting
    int: returned
    int: valued
    @: void result
end: LmxWalkFrame

prototype:
    # One activation of the callable occurrence `node`, for a DIRECT caller.  `args` are
    # `nargs` references (0 for an absent input); each one that is present must be an
    # address the context's arena can classify -- its own or its bound permanent store's --
    # otherwise LMX_WALK_INVALID and nothing runs.  `out` may be 0.  A turn run through a
    # Thread passes 0: what a turn has to say it says by a Message
    # (LMX_blog/2026-09-20.md, "Общение только через Message").
    fn: lmx_walk_run (@: LmxWalkContext context; @: Lmx node; @@: void args; size_t: nargs; @: LmxWalkValue out) int
    # The same activation for a caller INSIDE this unit: the inputs are values it produced
    # itself and are not asked about; an integer result goes into `dest`.
    fn: lmx_walk_activate (@: LmxWalkContext context; @: Lmx node; @@: void args; size_t: nargs; @: void dest; @@: void result; @: int present) int
    # Once per callable, in the arena that holds its descriptor and before the descriptor
    # is shared: look at the body, build the plan in `descriptor_arena`, publish it.  OK
    # without looking when the descriptor already has a plan.  A bare METHOD in child[0]
    # is INVALID (no header); a body with a node outside the slice is UNSUPPORTED and an
    # `own` node naming somebody else's field is INVALID -- in both cases nothing is
    # published.  NO_GRAPH when M has no body node.
    fn: lmx_walk_prepare (@: LmxWalkContext context; @: LmxArena descriptor_arena; @: Lmx plan_roles; @: Lmx node) int
    # Preparation and tests only: does the published plan say what the body says -- the
    # same fields, no more, in the lexical order?  The generic plan unit cannot know.
    fn: lmx_walk_plan_check (@: LmxWalkContext context; @: Lmx node) int
    # Entry, separate so that a test can look at what it loaded: two questions to the
    # arena, then the records of the plan's entries in the plan's order (21.5).  The
    # CALLER has marked the store and rewinds it; enter itself releases nothing.
    fn: lmx_walk_enter (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: LmxWalkContext context; @: Lmx node; @@: void args; size_t: nargs) int

    # The roles of a program: a plain Structure whose child k is THE record of role k.
    # Opened once per program graph; every frame node refers to these records.
    fn: lmx_walk_roles_open (@: LmxArena arena) @: Lmx
    # Construction of body nodes, for a translator and for tests.  A node is a fresh
    # Structure of `arena` with `children` slots and lexical parent `parent`; a frame
    # node gets the shared record of role `code` in child[0].  The caller stores the node
    # into its parent's slot.
    fn: lmx_walk_frame (@: LmxArena arena; @: Lmx roles; @: Lmx parent; int: code; size_t: children) @: Lmx
    fn: lmx_walk_plain (@: LmxArena arena; @: Lmx parent; size_t: children) @: Lmx
    fn: lmx_walk_store_size (@: LmxArena arena; @: Lmx code; size_t: slot; size_t: value) int
    fn: lmx_walk_store_int (@: LmxArena arena; @: Lmx code; size_t: slot; int: value) int

    # Internal steps, named here because they are mutually recursive.
    fn: lmx_walk_kind (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: void p) int
    fn: lmx_walk_op (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: void code) int
    fn: lmx_walk_scan (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: Lmx code; size_t: first) int
    fn: lmx_walk_eval (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: void code; @: void dest; @@: void out) int
    fn: lmx_walk_body (@: LmxWalkFrame f; @: Lmx body; size_t: first) int
    fn: lmx_walk_checkpoint (@: LmxWalkFrame f) int
end: prototype
